  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study measured bus-based transit access to nine types of essential opportunities in Hanoi, Vietnam, using open-source datasets. At a 60-minute travel threshold, citywide spatial coverage was low, ranging from 14.8% of hexagons for sports facilities to 29.1% for retail and grocery stores, with rural coverage peaking at 23.9%. The distribution of access was highly unequal and concentrated along transit corridors, yielding high population-weighted Gini coefficients between 0.603 and 0.654.",
      "why": "It provides empirical evidence of severe transit-based spatial inequality in a rapidly urbanizing Southeast Asian capital, quantifying a stark urban-rural divide and corridor-centric access patterns that were previously unmeasured."
    }
  },
